#### Article 7

1. Every woman has the right to obtain special services when in an emergency situation caused by:
   a. Women who become refugees;  
   b. Women victims of riots;  
   c. Women victims of natural disasters and non-natural disasters;  
   d. Women in armed conflict situations;  
   e. Women victims of terrorism networks;  
   f. Women victims of abduction, sale and victims of sexual exploitation;  
   g. Women victims of narcotics, alcohol, psychotropic, and other addictive substance abuse;  
   h. Women with HIV/AIDS.  
2. The Regional Government through the authorized Regional Apparatus, the community and the business sector must be actively involved in efforts to handle and fulfill the basic rights of women in emergency situations.  
3. The Regional Government may allocate special funds for handling emergency situations.

# CHAPTER IV

## OBLIGATIONS AND RESPONSIBILITIES

## OF THE REGIONAL GOVERNMENT

## Part One

General

#### Article 8

1. The Regional Government has the obligation and responsibility to provide empowerment and protection to women so that they can realize and enjoy a decent standard of living.  
2. Efforts to empower and protect women as referred to in paragraph (1), include:
   a. determination of policies, programs and activities;  
   b. determination of implementation guidelines;  
   c. provision of services by related Regional Apparatus;  
   d. coordination of policies, programs and activities; and  
   e. provision of facilities, Infrastructure and facilitation.

## Part Two

Determination of Policies, Programs and Activities

#### Article 9

1. The Regional Government facilitates the establishment of women's empowerment and protection institutions.  
2. The Mayor establishes policies, programs and activities for women's empowerment and protection to eliminate all forms of discrimination and violence against women in the Region.  
3. Implementation of policies, programs and activities for women's empowerment and protection as referred to in paragraph (2) is carried out by forming, developing, strengthening or utilizing task forces, forums, working groups or other institutions.  
4. Membership of task forces, forums, working groups or other institutions as referred to in paragraph (3) consists of related Regional Apparatus, community leaders, religious leaders, the business sector and the education sector.  
5. Further provisions regarding the establishment of task forces, forums, working groups or other institutions as referred to in paragraph (3) are regulated by the Mayor's Regulation.

## Part Three

Implementation of Policies, Programs and Activities

#### Article 10

Implementation of policies, programs and activities for women's empowerment and protection, may be carried out through efforts to:

a. cooperate with the business sector, non-governmental institutions, private companies, foundations, the education sector and other domestic institutions as well as international institutions, in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations;  
b. conduct regional cooperation, in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations;  
c. provide and facilitate women's empowerment and protection services;  
d. compile a data system for women's empowerment and protection;  
e. compile prevention programs through comprehensive education and socialization in order to prevent violence against women; and  
f. implement coordination of the implementation of policies, programs and activities for women's empowerment and protection among Regional Apparatus.

## Part Four

Provision of Facilities, Infrastructure and Facilitation

#### Article 11

1. Provision of facilities, infrastructure and facilitation of women's protection services as referred to in Article 8 paragraph (2) letter e in the form of:
   a. victim identification;  
   b. legal assistance;  
   c. medical rehabilitation;  
   d. psychosocial rehabilitation;  
   e. social reintegration;  
   f. repatriation assistance;  
   g. providing safe houses; or  
   h. other forms.  
2. Provision of facilities, infrastructure and facilitation of women's protection services as referred to in paragraph (1) is provided to women:
   a. victims of violence;  
   b. victims of human trafficking;  
   c. elderly who are not independent;  
   d. with disabilities;  
   e. victims of terrorism networks;  
   f. victims of natural and non-natural disasters;  
   g. refugee women;  
   h. victims of sexual exploitation;  
   i. women heads of households;  
   j. conflict and disaster prone areas;  
   k. women workers; and  
   l. other vulnerable groups.

# CHAPTER V

## WOMEN'S EMPOWERMENT

## Part One

General

#### Article 12

1. The Regional Government has the obligation and responsibility in efforts to empower women.  
2. Efforts to empower women as referred to in paragraph (1) are carried out in an integrated manner in accordance with laws and regulations.  
3. Women's empowerment as referred to in paragraph (1) is directed to obtain opportunities and rights as human beings to be able to play a role and participate in the fields of:
   a. economy;  
   b. socio-culture;  
   c. politics and government;  
   d. law;  
   e. education;  
   f. health;  
   g. employment; and  
   h. social security.

## Part Two

Empowerment in the Economic Field

#### Article 13

1. Women's empowerment in the economic field as referred to in Article 12 paragraph (3) letter a is implemented by Regional Apparatus in charge of economy, employment, industry and trade, welfare, tourism and culture, cooperatives and MSMEs, individuals, the community, the education sector and the business sector.  
2. Implementation of Women's Empowerment in the economic field as referred to in paragraph (1) is carried out through:
   a. job training;  
   b. productive economic enterprises and joint economic enterprises;  
   c. capital assistance and employment accessibility;  
   d. marketing network development facilities.  
3. Job training as referred to in paragraph (2) letter a includes:
   a. briefing, workshops, mentoring for women to be ready to work;  
   b. field work practice; and  
   c. apprenticeship.  
4. Productive economic enterprises and joint business groups as referred to in paragraph (2) letter b include:
   a. entrepreneurship skills training;  
   b. facilitation of the formation of joint business groups; and  
   c. business implementation assistance.  
5. Capital assistance and employment accessibility is the responsibility of regional financial institutions facilitated by the Regional Apparatus in charge of cooperatives and micro, small and medium enterprises as referred to in (2) letter c includes:
   a. assistance with work facilities and infrastructure;  
   b. facilitating working capital assistance by the Regional Apparatus in charge of cooperatives and micro, small and medium enterprises; and  
   c. employment accessibility that is fair for women.  
6. Marketing network development facilities as referred to in paragraph (2) letter d include:
   a. marketing assistance for women's business products through government programs;  
   b. facilitating the marketing of women's business products domestically and abroad;  
   c. involving women's business groups to introduce their business products.

## Part Three

Empowerment in the Socio-Cultural Field

